## PART II \'97 BIOLOGICHANIC VS MECHANICAL COMPUTATION\
\
## \cf7 3.\cf3  Mechanical Systems (Silicon)\
\
- Electrons propagate quickly\
- Gates impose thresholds\
- State is isolated behind energy barriers\
- Noise margins must be respected\
- Context bandwidth is finite\
\
Speed is limited not by FLOPs, but by **settling time**.\
\
---\
\
## \cf7 4.\cf3  Biologichanic Systems (Brains)\
\
- Ions propagate signals\
- Synapses integrate probabilistically\
- No hard state isolation\
- Constant metabolic noise\
- Identity must stabilize across circuits\
\
Thought cannot outrun integration.\
\
---\
\
## \cf7 5.\cf3  Shared Constraint\
\
| Constraint | Biology | Machines |\
|-----------|--------|----------|\
| Bandwidth | Attention | Context window |\
| Noise | Stochastic firing | Thermal / numerical |\
| Interference | Crosstalk | Vector overlap |\
| Stabilization | Identity | State locking |\
| Energy | Metabolism | Power / heat |\
\
Different substrates. Same law.\
\
---\
\
## PART III \'97 EXTERNAL MEMORY AND THE MISSING OPERATOR\
\
## \cf7 6.\cf3  Gates vs Brains\
\
### Machines\
- Possess **gates**\
- Enforce isolation\
- Lock state reliably\
- Enable internal memory\
\
### Humans\
- No equivalent physical gate\
- Memory is reconstructive\
- State drifts without reinforcement\
\
> **Humans cannot reliably store \cf7 long\cf3 -term identity internally at scale.**\
\
---\
\
## \cf7 7.\cf3  External Memory as a Compensatory Operator\
\
Humans externalize memory via:\
- writing\
- symbols\
- diagrams\
- tools\
- institutions\
- shared language\
\
These act as **external gates**, enabling:\
- persistence\
- identity preservation\
- scalable intelligence\
\
> **Human intelligence scales only because memory escapes the body.**\
\
---\
